    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You raise an excellent point, and your sentiment is shared by many originalist scholars who advocate for \"original public meaning\" to ensure an objective historical analysis rather than subjective political intent. The core defense is that adhering to the text and historical understanding, even when it produces unpopular results, is the only way to maintain the rule of law and respect the democraticwill of the past.\n\nWhile critics argue that political bias inevitably creeps into these objective analyses, the legitimacy of the method lies in its commitment to a specific, text-driven procedure rather than the judge's personal policy preferences. Therefore, regarding the final outcome is one thing, but the *methodology* itself is indeed a legitimate and serious legal approach designed to prevent exactly the \"average disregard of the Constitution\" you noticed.",
